/-!
# Torsion-free realization of a quasi-coherent inverse system

Let `X` be a scheme, let `f` act on sheaves of modules on `X` by multiplication by a global
section of the structure sheaf, and let `… → ℱ₃ → ℱ₂ → ℱ₁` be an inverse system of
quasi-coherent `𝒪_X`-modules. The successive short-exact-sequence condition on the system is
equivalent to realizing every `ℱ_n` as `ℱ / fⁿℱ` for a single `f`-torsion-free module `ℱ`.

Three of the four equivalent conditions of the source, and the implication from the
torsion-free realization to the others, hold over an arbitrary sheaf of rings and are the
content of Stacks 0H39. What is added here is the converse implication, which uses both the
scheme structure and quasi-coherence: the limit `ℱ = lim ℱ_n` realizes the system because the
subsheaf `fⁿℱ` is the ideal-power multiple `I ⁿ ℱ` for the ideal sheaf `I = (f)`, so that
Stacks 0EI8 identifies `ℱ / fⁿℱ` with `ℱ_n`.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.Algebraization.scalarIdealData` — the quasi-coherent ideal sheaf generated by the
  global section through which `f` acts.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Algebraization.condition_one_iff_four_of_quasicoherent` — the equivalence of the
  short-exact-sequence condition with the torsion-free realization.
* `Stacks.Algebraization.condition_one_iff_four_of_quasicoherent_globalSection` — the same
  equivalence for multiplication by a genuine global section of `𝒪_X`.
* `Stacks.Algebraization.conditions_tfae_of_quasicoherent` — all four conditions of the
  source are equivalent, the remaining equivalences coming from Stacks 0H39.
* `Stacks.Algebraization.conditions_tfae_of_quasicoherent_globalSection` — the same four-way
  equivalence for multiplication by a genuine global section of `𝒪_X`.
* `Stacks.Algebraization.principalPowerQuotientIso` — for a principal ideal sheaf `(f)` the
  ideal-power quotient `M / (f)ⁿ M` is the cokernel of multiplication by `fⁿ`.

## Implementation notes

Multiplication by a global section is represented by an endomorphism of the identity functor on
sheaves of modules, which loses nothing: every such endomorphism is multiplication by a global
section, by `Stacks.SheafOfModules.scalarActionLocal`. The inverse system is indexed from zero,
its term `n` being the source's `ℱ_{n+1}`, which is the convention of both Stacks 0H39 and
`Stacks.Algebraization.AdicInverseSystem`.

The two presentations `X.Modules` and `SheafOfModules X.ringCatSheaf` of the category of sheaves
of modules carry separately declared category instances, and the scalar action on sections is
declared over `X.ringCatSheaf.obj.obj U` in one place and over `Γ(X, U)` in another; the
`local instance`s below restate existing instances at the spelling the elaborator meets.

The same two presentations explain two shapes in the main proof. The data the inverse limit
supplies — the limit object, its projections and the reduction isomorphisms — is extracted by a
single `obtain` whose type names each of them at the `X.Modules` presentation, so that the rest
of the argument never has to mediate between the two. And `mono_app_of_jointly_mono` is stated
for an abstract jointly monic family rather than for `limit` itself, since `HasLimit` is only
available for `X.Modules`, while multiplication by `f` forces the other presentation.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0EH9](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0EH9)
-/

@[expose] public section

noncomputable section

universe u

